An energy company runs hundreds of batteries with different owners as one fleet and sells the fleet's capacity on the reserve market. The market pays the fleet as a whole. Every month someone at the company splits the money between the owners and has to be able to explain the split to each of them.

# The evening hour

Let's say the fleet has two batteries that each hold 1 MWh. Battery A sits at a solar park and is 90 % full after a sunny afternoon. At a factory, battery B has run down to 20 % covering the factory's peak.

![A battery storage system in two containers in Rheineck, Switzerland. Batteries A and B would sit in containers like these at the solar park and the factory, and 1 MWh fills only part of one container. Photo: Kecko, [CC BY 2.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/), via [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tesvolt_battery_energy_storage_system_Rheineck.jpg).](battery-site.webp){#fig-battery-site}

In the evening the fleet sells FCR-N, a frequency reserve that Fingrid, the Finnish grid operator, buys to keep the grid at 50 Hz. FCR-N is symmetric, so every MW sold has to be ready both to discharge when the frequency drops and to charge when it rises. A battery has to hold enough energy to deliver its full bid for an hour in either direction [@fingrid_fcr].

Upwards a battery is limited by its charge and downwards by its empty space, so it sells the smaller of the two. A full battery sells nothing and a half-full one sells 0.5 MW. Our two batteries are far from half full, so on its own A sells 0.1 MW and B sells 0.2 MW.

In a fleet the controller can send every upward order to the batteries that have charge and every downward order to the ones that have space. The fleet then sells the smaller of its total charge and its total space. With A's charge on the upward side and B's space on the downward side, the two together sell 0.9 MW. Fingrid checks the hour-long rule for the fleet as a whole. Batteries at different sites can qualify as one reserve provider, and each battery on its own does not have to meet the rule [@fingrid_fcr]. We also assume that the batteries' power is never the limit.

Revenue is the reserve price times the MW sold, so splitting the money is the same as splitting the 0.9 MW. The question is who gets the extra 0.6 MW that neither battery could sell alone. The solar park's owner says that every upward MW the fleet sold came from A. The factory's owner answers that the fleet ran out of space before it ran out of charge, and nearly all of that space was in B.

Before you look at the two rows below your split, set the split you think is fair with the last slider.

