```{js}
//| label: fig-evening-hour
//| input: soc_a, soc_b, share_a
//| fig-cap: "Top: each battery's charge (what it can offer upwards) and empty space (what it can offer downwards), and the fleet's totals. The line marks what each sells, the smaller of the two bars. Bottom: three splits of the fleet's sale, A from the left and B from the right. The green band holds the splits where neither owner would earn more alone."

// slider values arrive as strings, or undefined before the inputs register
const read = (name, fallback) => {
  const x = Number(tali.value(name));
  return Number.isFinite(x) ? x : fallback;
};
const pa = read("soc_a", 90);
const pb = read("soc_b", 20);
const share = read("share_a", 50) / 100;

// whole percents, so a tie between charge and space is an exact comparison
const fleetChargePct = pa + pb;
const fleetSpacePct = 200 - pa - pb;
const aloneA = Math.min(pa, 100 - pa) / 100;
const aloneB = Math.min(pb, 100 - pb) / 100;
const total = Math.min(fleetChargePct, fleetSpacePct) / 100;

// shapley: own sale plus half the extra
const shapleyA = aloneA + (total - aloneA - aloneB) / 2;

// dual prices: pay for the side that runs out; at a tie any mix of the two is optimal
let dualLo, dualHi;
if (fleetSpacePct < fleetChargePct) {
  dualLo = dualHi = (100 - pa) / 100;
} else if (fleetChargePct < fleetSpacePct) {
  dualLo = dualHi = pa / 100;
} else {
  dualLo = Math.min(pa, 100 - pa) / 100;
  dualHi = Math.max(pa, 100 - pa) / 100;
}
const yourA = share * total;
const coreLo = aloneA;
const coreHi = total - aloneB;

// top panel: charge and space bars
const bars = [
  ["A", pa / 100, 1 - pa / 100],
  ["B", pb / 100, 1 - pb / 100],
  ["Fleet", fleetChargePct / 100, fleetSpacePct / 100],
].flatMap(([who, c, s]) => [
  { who, kind: "charge", value: c },
  { who, kind: "space", value: s },
]);
const sells = [
  { who: "A", sells: aloneA },
  { who: "B", sells: aloneB },
  { who: "Fleet", sells: total },
];
const top = Plot.plot({
  width: 640,
  height: 230,
  marginTop: 24,
  fx: {
    domain: ["A", "B", "Fleet"],
    label: null,
    tickFormat: (d) => (d === "Fleet" ? "Fleet (A + B)" : `Battery ${d}`),
  },
  x: { domain: ["charge", "space"], label: null, tickFormat: (d) => (d === "charge" ? "charge ↑" : "space ↓") },
  y: { domain: [0, 2], label: "MWh", grid: true },
  color: { domain: ["charge", "space"], range: ["gold", "#6b7280"] },
  marks: [
    Plot.barY(bars, { fx: "who", x: "kind", y: "value", fill: "kind" }),
    Plot.ruleY(sells, { fx: "who", y: "sells", stroke: "currentColor", strokeWidth: 2 }),
    Plot.text(sells, {
      fx: "who",
      text: (d) => `sells ${d.sells.toFixed(2)} MW`,
      frameAnchor: "top",
      dy: 2,
      fill: "currentColor",
    }),
  ],
});

// bottom panel: three splits against the core band
const rows = [
  ["Your split", yourA, yourA],
  ["Shapley", shapleyA, shapleyA],
  ["Dual prices", dualLo, dualHi],
];
const segments = rows.flatMap(([row, lo, hi]) => [
  { row, owner: "A", x1: 0, x2: lo },
  { row, owner: "either", x1: lo, x2: hi },
  { row, owner: "B", x1: hi, x2: total },
]).filter((d) => d.x2 - d.x1 > 1e-9);
const bottom = Plot.plot({
  width: 640,
  height: 170,
  marginLeft: 90,
  x: { domain: [0, Math.max(total, 0.05)], label: "MW credited →" },
  y: { domain: rows.map((r) => r[0]), label: null },
  color: { domain: ["A", "B", "either"], range: ["steelblue", "darkorange", "#9ca3af"] },
  marks: [
    Plot.rect([{ x1: coreLo, x2: coreHi }], { x1: "x1", x2: "x2", fill: "mediumseagreen", fillOpacity: 0.3 }),
    Plot.barX(segments, { x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y: "row", fill: "owner", insetTop: 7, insetBottom: 7 }),
    Plot.ruleX([coreLo, coreHi], { stroke: "mediumseagreen", strokeWidth: 2, strokeDasharray: "4,3" }),
  ],
});

// your split against what each owner sells alone
const f = (x) => x.toFixed(2);
let verdict;
if (total === 0) {
  verdict = "The fleet sells nothing in this hour, so there is nothing to split.";
} else if (yourA < aloneA - 1e-9) {
  verdict = `A gets ${f(yourA)} MW but sells ${f(aloneA)} MW alone, so A's owner would leave.`;
} else if (total - yourA < aloneB - 1e-9) {
  verdict = `B gets ${f(total - yourA)} MW but sells ${f(aloneB)} MW alone, so B's owner would leave.`;
} else {
  verdict = "Each owner gets at least what the battery sells alone, so both stay.";
}
const dualText = dualLo === dualHi ? `A ${f(dualLo)}, B ${f(total - dualLo)}` : `A between ${f(dualLo)} and ${f(dualHi)}`;
const text = document.createElement("p");
text.style.cssText = "font-size:0.9em;margin:0.4em 0 0;";
text.textContent = `Your split: A ${f(yourA)}, B ${f(total - yourA)}. ${verdict} Shapley: A ${f(shapleyA)}, B ${f(total - shapleyA)}. Dual prices: ${dualText}.`;

const out = document.createElement("div");
out.append(top, bottom, text);
return out;
```

Paying each battery for the energy it holds is a natural first guess. It gives A 0.74 MW and B 0.16 MW. B sells 0.2 MW on its own, so the factory's owner would take B out of the fleet, and A would be left selling 0.1 MW. For both owners to stay, A's share has to lie inside the green band in @fig-evening-hour.

The lower two rows split the sale by Shapley values and by dual prices. Both land inside the band, the dual split on its left edge, and they differ by 0.3 MW in what they give A.

With equal charges in both batteries, the two rules split the sale evenly. If A is at 60 % and B at 10 %, the fleet has more space than charge, and the dual prices pay for charge instead of space. Raising A to 90 % makes the fleet's charge equal its space. The dual row then turns grey, and any split in the grey stretch is an equally valid answer.

# Two ways to split

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Notation used in this post

| Symbol | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| $c_i$ | Charge of battery $i$ in MWh, also its upward capacity in MW |
| $1 - c_i$ | Empty space of battery $i$, its downward capacity |
| $S$ | A group of batteries |
| $v(S)$ | MW the group $S$ sells on its own |
| $x_i$ | MW credited to battery $i$ |
| $\pi$ | An order in which the batteries join the fleet |
| $P_i^\pi$ | The batteries that join before $i$ in the order $\pi$ |
| $\phi_i$ | Shapley value of battery $i$ |
| $b$ | The fleet's bid in MW |
| $u_i, d_i$ | Upward and downward capacity battery $i$ offers |
| $\lambda, \mu$ | Dual prices of one MWh of charge and one MWh of space |
:::

The math below uses the evening hour with the demo's starting charges. Each battery holds 1 MWh.

::: {}
| Battery | Site | Charge $c_i$ | Space $1 - c_i$ |
|---------|------|--------------|-----------------|
| A | Solar park | 0.9 MWh | 0.1 MWh |
| B | Factory | 0.2 MWh | 0.8 MWh |
| Fleet | Both | 1.1 MWh | 0.9 MWh |
:::

A group of batteries sells the smaller of its total charge and its total space:

$$v(S) = \min\Big(\sum_{i \in S} c_i,\; \sum_{i \in S} (1 - c_i)\Big)$$

In the evening hour this gives

$$v(\{A\}) = 0.1, \qquad v(\{B\}) = 0.2, \qquad v(\{A, B\}) = 0.9$$

A split keeps both owners in the fleet when each gets at least what its battery sells alone and the shares add up to the fleet's sale:

$$x_A \ge v(\{A\}), \qquad x_B \ge v(\{B\}), \qquad x_A + x_B = v(\{A, B\})$$

The set of such splits is called the core, and in the demo it is the green band.

## Shapley values

The Shapley value [@shapley1953] lets the batteries join the fleet one at a time and credits each with what the fleet gains when it joins. It then averages that credit over every joining order:

$$\phi_i = \frac{1}{n!} \sum_{\pi} \Big( v\big(P_i^\pi \cup \{i\}\big) - v\big(P_i^\pi\big) \Big)$$

With two batteries there are two orders. A adds little when it joins an empty fleet and a lot when B is there first:

$$\begin{aligned}
\phi_A &= \tfrac{1}{2}\big(\underbrace{0.1}_{\text{A first}} + \underbrace{0.9 - 0.2}_{\text{A after B}}\big) = 0.4 \\
\phi_B &= \tfrac{1}{2}\big(\underbrace{0.2}_{\text{B first}} + \underbrace{0.9 - 0.1}_{\text{B after A}}\big) = 0.5
\end{aligned}$$

For two batteries this is the same as each keeping its own sale and taking half of the extra.

## Dual prices

The second rule comes from writing the fleet's sale as a linear program, a linear objective maximised under linear limits. Each battery offers upward capacity $u_i \le c_i$ and downward capacity $d_i \le 1 - c_i$, and the fleet's bid $b$ has to be covered in both directions:

$$\begin{aligned}
\max_{b,\, u,\, d} \quad & b \\
\text{subject to} \quad & b \le \sum_i u_i, \quad b \le \sum_i d_i \\
& u_i \le c_i, \quad d_i \le 1 - c_i
\end{aligned}$$

Every linear program comes with a second one, called its dual. The dual puts a price on each limit. Here the limits are charge and space, so the dual prices a MWh of charge at $\lambda$ and a MWh of space at $\mu$. One price per side is enough, since a MWh of charge is worth the same in either battery.

Selling 1 MW for the hour takes one MWh of charge and one MWh of space. Together the two have to be priced at no less than the 1 MW they sell, so $\lambda + \mu \ge 1$. The dual looks for the prices that put the lowest bill on the fleet's capacity:

$$\begin{aligned}
\min_{\lambda,\, \mu \ge 0} \quad & \lambda \sum_i c_i + \mu \sum_i (1 - c_i) \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \lambda + \mu \ge 1
\end{aligned}$$

Prices that add up to more than 1 only raise the bill, so the cheapest ones add up to exactly 1. In the evening hour the fleet holds 1.1 MWh of charge and 0.9 MWh of space, and with $\mu = 1 - \lambda$ its capacity is worth

$$1.1\,\lambda + 0.9\,(1 - \lambda) = 0.9 + 0.2\,\lambda$$

The bill is lowest at $\lambda = 0$. There charge is free and space costs 1 per MWh. The fleet's capacity is then worth 0.9 MW, exactly what it sells. @fig-dual-prices draws the bill for every price of charge, along with what the same prices pay A and B for their own capacity.

```{js}
//| label: fig-dual-prices
//| fig-cap: "What the evening hour's batteries are paid when a MWh of charge costs λ and a MWh of space costs 1 − λ. Each solid line is what a battery or the fleet is paid, and the dashed line of the same colour is what it sells. No payment drops below its dashed line, and at λ = 0 the fleet's payment equals its sale."

// evening hour: A at 90 %, B at 20 %, capacity in MWh
const groups = [
  { who: "Fleet", charge: 1.1, space: 0.9, sells: 0.9, color: "currentColor" },
  { who: "A", charge: 0.9, space: 0.1, sells: 0.1, color: "steelblue" },
  { who: "B", charge: 0.2, space: 0.8, sells: 0.2, color: "darkorange" },
];
const prices = d3.range(0, 1.0001, 0.01);
const paid = groups.flatMap((g) =>
  prices.map((lambda) => ({ ...g, lambda, paid: g.charge * lambda + g.space * (1 - lambda) }))
);
// dashed-line labels sit where no payment line crosses them, offset in MW
const floorLabels = [
  { ...groups[0], at: 0.5, offset: -0.05, label: "Fleet sells 0.9" },
  { ...groups[1], at: 0.8, offset: 0.04, label: "A sells 0.1 alone" },
  { ...groups[2], at: 0.45, offset: 0.04, label: "B sells 0.2 alone" },
];

return Plot.plot({
  width: 640,
  height: 280,
  marginRight: 50,
  x: { domain: [0, 1], label: "price of a MWh of charge, λ (space costs 1 − λ) →" },
  y: { domain: [0, 1.15], label: "MW paid", grid: true },
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY(groups, { y: "sells", stroke: "color", strokeWidth: 1.5, strokeDasharray: "4,3" }),
    Plot.text(floorLabels, {
      x: "at",
      y: (d) => d.sells + d.offset,
      text: "label",
      fill: "color",
      fontSize: 11,
    }),
    Plot.line(paid, { x: "lambda", y: "paid", z: "who", stroke: "color", strokeWidth: 2 }),
    Plot.text(groups, {
      x: 1,
      y: (d) => d.charge,
      text: "who",
      dx: 8,
      textAnchor: "start",
      fill: "color",
    }),
    Plot.dot([{ lambda: 0, paid: 0.9 }], { x: "lambda", y: "paid", r: 4, fill: "currentColor" }),
  ],
});
```

No prices in the figure value the fleet below what it sells. This is called weak duality. Strong duality says that the cheapest prices value it at exactly what it sells. Both hold for every linear program.

The cheapest prices also say what one more MWh of each limit is worth. The fleet runs out of space first, so one more MWh of space would let it sell one more MW, and $\mu = 1$. One more MWh of charge would add nothing, and $\lambda = 0$. Prices with this meaning are also called shadow prices.

The dual rule pays each battery for its own capacity at the cheapest prices:

$$x_i = \lambda c_i + \mu (1 - c_i)$$

At these prices each battery is paid for its space:

$$x_A = 1 - c_A = 0.1, \qquad x_B = 1 - c_B = 0.8$$

The figure also shows why this split is in the core. With $\lambda + \mu = 1$, the payment to a battery, or to any group of batteries, is a weighted average of its charge and its space. An average is never below the smaller of the two numbers, and the smaller one is what the group sells on its own. So the A and B lines never drop below their dashed lines, and at the cheapest prices the shares add up to the fleet's sale. Owen proved the same for every linear program of this form, where each owner brings resources and a group sells what its pooled resources allow [@owen1975].

## Where each rule breaks

When the fleet's charge equals its space, its line in @fig-dual-prices is flat, and the dual split depends on which prices the solver returns. This is the grey stretch in the demo.

The Shapley value always lands in the core with two batteries and can fall outside it with three. The cell below solves the linear program with CVXPY for every group of a three-battery fleet and compares both splits with what each group sells on its own.

```{python}
#| code-fold: true
#| code-summary: "CVXPY: solve every group, then compare both splits"
import itertools
import math

import cvxpy as cp
import numpy as np

charge = np.array([0.95, 0.15, 0.60])  # MWh stored in three 1 MWh batteries
names = ["A", "B", "C"]
n = len(charge)


def fleet_sale(group):
    """MW of FCR-N the batteries in `group` sell together, and their dual prices."""
    inside = np.isin(np.arange(n), list(group)).astype(float)
    up = cp.Variable(n, nonneg=True)
    down = cp.Variable(n, nonneg=True)
    bid = cp.Variable(nonneg=True)
    has_charge = up <= inside * charge
    has_space = down <= inside * (1 - charge)
    problem = cp.Problem(
        cp.Maximize(bid),
        [bid <= cp.sum(up), bid <= cp.sum(down), has_charge, has_space],
    )
    problem.solve()
    return problem.value, has_charge.dual_value, has_space.dual_value


groups = [frozenset(g) for r in range(1, n + 1) for g in itertools.combinations(range(n), r)]
v = {g: fleet_sale(g)[0] for g in groups}
v[frozenset()] = 0.0

# shapley: average what each battery adds over every joining order
shapley = np.zeros(n)
for i in range(n):
    for g in v:
        if i not in g:
            weight = math.factorial(len(g)) * math.factorial(n - len(g) - 1) / math.factorial(n)
            shapley[i] += weight * (v[g | {i}] - v[g])

# dual split: each battery's charge and space at the fleet's dual prices
total, price_charge, price_space = fleet_sale(range(n))
dual = price_charge * charge + price_space * (1 - charge)

print("group   sells alone   Shapley gives   dual gives")
for g in groups:
    members = sorted(g)
    label = "+".join(names[i] for i in members)
    s, d = shapley[members].sum(), dual[members].sum()
    flag = "  <- would leave" if s < v[g] - 1e-6 else ""
    print(f"{label:7} {v[g]:11.3f} {s:15.3f} {d:12.3f}{flag}")
```

In the A+B row, the Shapley shares of A and B add up to less than the two batteries sell as a pair. Their owners would do better leaving together. The dual split gives every group at least what it sells on its own.

Exact Shapley values need $v(S)$ for every group. A fleet of 300 batteries has $2^{300}$ groups, far too many to solve a linear program for each. In practice the Shapley value is estimated by sampling joining orders [@castro2009]. Dual prices need one solve.

# What the owners get

Both rules keep the solar park's and the factory's owners in the fleet. The Shapley value gives A credit for making B's space sellable. Dual prices pay only for the capacity the fleet is short of. In the evening hour that is space, so B gets most of the sale.

In a fleet of hundreds, Shapley values can only be estimated, and they can push a group of owners to leave. The dual split takes one solve, and no group gets less than it sells alone.
